      "title": "Finding Regions Where a Transformation is Orientation-Preserving or Reversing"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1b46114a-aaf4-5c5c-bc37-232967d9b231",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\left(u - 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ v - 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202(x,y)}{\u2202(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\u27f6\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\left(u - 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ v - 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202(x,y)}{\u2202(u,v)} &=\\begin{vmatrix}4 u - 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(4 u - 4)\\cdot (1)-(0)\\cdot (0) \\\\ &=4 u - 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 u - 4 &<0 \\\\ u < 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation\n\n$$\nu = 1\n$$\n\ndefines a vertical line.\n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-28-topic-audit-3/published-probe/17592186129283/plot-f44176179c372cf7cd68.png)",
